AB  - Accurate estimation of root-zone soil moisture and soil hydraulic parameters is essential for simulating water and energy fluxes, optimizing agricultural water use, and improving drought and flood forecasts. These quantities are difficult to measure directly but can be inferred by combining hydrologic models with remotely sensed near-surface soil moisture. Variational data assimilation (VDA) provides a rigorous framework for this purpose but often requires computationally expensive full-model adjoints. The Reduced-Adjoint Variational Data Assimilation (RA-VDA) framework overcomes this limitation by integrating Proper Orthogonal Decomposition (POD) model reduction with a reduced-order adjoint, making it model-agnostic and computationally efficient. This study extends RA-VDA to coupled state–parameter estimation and evaluates its robustness under structural, initial condition, and forcing uncertainties using HYDRUS-1D. Results show that RA-VDA maintains accuracy while remaining computationally tractable and demonstrating strong resilience to errors, establishing its potential for large-scale environmental modeling without requiring full-model adjoint codes. Importantly, RA-VDA's model-agnostic design makes it directly applicable to any existing hydrologic or land surface model without internal code modification, offering a practical and scalable path toward satellite-driven soil moisture and hydraulic parameter estimation at regional and global scales.
