AB  - Accidental or malevolent atmospheric releases are feared as they pose a serious threat to human health and the environment. We first outline the gap between advanced atmospheric transport and dispersion (AT&D) models developed by the scientific community, and the oversimplified models present in decision-support systems (DSSs). We then describe a modelling system compromising between the detailed resolution of the AT&D equations and the fast delivery of operational results. We illustrate this approach through the “EMERGENCIES Méditerranée” project and demonstrate the possibility of producing micrometeorological forecasts that are ready to be used in emergencies, as well as meter-scale dispersion simulations, on demand and within accelerated time. The study areas cover megacities along the French Mediterranean coast. The calculations, carried out on powerful but affordable computing resources, represent a world first. Finally, we discuss the overall concept of the system developed, arguing that it could revolutionize preparedness and response to emergency situations.
