AB  - Accurate water-quality prediction is essential for sustainable aquaculture. However, multivariate time series are highly coupled, nonstationary, and multiscale, posing challenges for long-term modeling. This study proposes AMAformer, a Transformer-based model for aquaculture forecasting. First, Adaptive Multi-kernel Temporal Decomposition separates trend and seasonal components to mitigate baseline drift. Second, Adaptive fractional Fourier-domain spectral Attention uses learnable fractional Fourier projections to capture nonstationary spectral peaks and cross-frequency dependencies. Third, Adaptive Channel–Temporal Hybrid Gating integrates channel-wise and temporal attention for modeling cross-variable and long-range dependencies. Experiments on six searanch datasets demonstrate that AMAformer outperforms eight baselines, reducing the mean absolute error (MAE) and root mean square error (RMSE) by 33.67% and 26.49% relative to the best-performing baseline, and improving coefficient of determination (R2) and Kling–Gupta Efficiency (KGE) by 9.22% and 4.56%, respectively. These results confirm its robustness and accuracy, offering an effective tool for aquaculture water-quality and risk management.
