AB  - This study presents a Python-based static flood model (PyFlood) that rapidly generates high resolution coastal flood maps using digital elevation models, land cover, and water level data. PyFlood reduces systematic overestimation in static models through four advances: a hybrid hydro-connectivity algorithm integrating topography and land cover, spatially varying water levels estimated by kriging with external drift using topography and bathymetry, attenuation distance computed from all permanent water bodies, and land-cover-based attenuation calibrated with high water marks using Bayesian optimization. The model was calibrated and validated in the Houston–Galveston region of Texas for Hurricane Ike (2008), and its transferability was evaluated using high water marks from Hurricane Harvey (2017). Results show strong agreement with a physics-based numerical model, substantially lower root mean square error against high water marks than an existing static model (0.507 m versus 3.412 m), and high computational efficiency, supporting emergency forecasting and response during coastal floods.
