AB  - Explainable AI (XAI) is a rapidly expanding field, with methods such as SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP becoming a standard for feature attribution. However, existing approaches face challenges related to limited explanatory power, computational efficiency, scalability, and sensitivity to sampling variability. We have recently developed Explainer VARS (XVARS), a novel XAI framework grounded in global sensitivity analysis, designed to overcome these challenges in a model-agnostic manner. In this paper, we comprehensively compare XVARS with Kernel and Tree SHAPs across multiple machine learning (ML) methods to assess their relative characteristics and performance. Results demonstrate that XVARS requires less than 10% of the computational time needed by SHAP for typical ML problems, consistent with theoretical expectations: XVARS computations are essentially independent of training sample size, whereas both SHAP-based approaches scale linearly with it. We also find that whereas Tree SHAP demonstrates higher efficiency than XVARS for moderate-sized datasets, its performance remains dependent on data volume and different tree model structures and, since the scalability threshold at which its computational cost exceeds that of XVARS is not yet well understood, we leave that as an open question. Meanwhile, XVARS exhibits greater robustness to sampling variability and yields more reliable feature importance estimates, owing to its characterization of the entire input–output space globally rather than focusing only on local neighbourhoods around observed samples, as in SHAPs.
