AB  - Contamination events such as spills and post-fire runoff affect water quality in streams and rivers, and physically based solute transport modeling has been used to predict how such events propagate downstream. However, predicting contaminant transport in these ecosystems remains challenging because model parameters cannot be consistently predicted using readily available site characteristics, such as width, depth, or discharge, through analytical or empirical equations. As a result, standard modeling approaches rely on site- and flow-specific a posteriori calibrations that effectively reproduce past events, but offer limited predictive capabilities. We present a machine learning approach for predicting conservative solute breakthrough curves (BTCs) using a multilayer perceptron (MLP) trained on synthetic and experimental data. To support this effort, we first developed the Tracer Injection Experiments in Rivers and Streams (TIERRAS) database, the largest compilation of conservative solute transport experiments across contrasting hydrogeomorphic characteristics to date. We augmented TIERRAS with synthetic BTCs generated from realistically constrained solutions of the one-dimensional advection-dispersion equation. The MLP model accurately captured solute transport dynamics, requiring ∼1000 synthetic, noise-free BTCs to achieve a cross-correlation score above 0.95 during training. Fine-tuning an MLP pretrained on 3000 synthetic BTCs with 121 experimental BTCs yielded the best generalization, achieving a cross-correlation score of 0.72 for unseen experimental data. These results highlight the potential of deep learning for robust, site-independent, and real-time prediction of solute transport processes, as well as the importance of expanding experimental databases, such as TIERRAS, to support future advances.
