AB  - Mountainous regions face increasing landslide risks due to climate change-driven extreme rainfall. Existing deep learning methods often fall short in capturing the complex hydro-mechanical processes and soil-specific hydrological responses leading to these failures. To address this, we developed a novel hybrid CNN-BiLSTM model, enhanced with an attention mechanism and a dynamic, soil-aware rainfall intensity-duration (EI-D) threshold. A key innovation is the refined EI-D model, which incorporates soil-specific decay coefficients (λ) to calculate effective cumulative rainfall intensity (Ieff=∑t=116rte−λ(T−t)). This differentiates the influence of antecedent rainfall in clay (λ = 0.15) versus sandy (λ = 0.30) soils, reflecting their differing hydraulic conductivities. The deep learning framework integrates static geospatial data (12 conditioning factors) with dynamic 16-day time-series data (rainfall, soil moisture) to capture both spatial patterns and bidirectional temporal dependencies. The attention mechanism focuses on the most critical time periods. We validated the model using 366 landslides triggered by the extreme rainfall event in Pingjiang County, China, in June 2024. The integrated model achieved strong performance: AUC-ROC of 0.888 ± 0.009, F1-score of 0.822 ± 0.010, precision of 0.837 ± 0.008, recall of 0.807 ± 0.012, and accuracy of 0.862 ± 0.009. This significantly outperformed CNN-only and Random Forest models. The soil-aware EI-D model improved landslide prediction by approximately 30%, capturing 59.1% of landslides compared to 45.4% with a traditional uniform-threshold model. Furthermore, our coupled warning system correctly identified 80% of landslides within the highest-risk zones. This framework offers a promising approach for climate-adaptive landslide early warning.
