AB  - Urban flood management requires agile regulation of distributed small-scale storage. This study presents a Dynamic Adaptive Model Predictive Control (DAMPC) framework using an Urban Flood Hazard Weighting Score (UFHWS) module. By reconfiguring multi-objective optimization into state-driven, single-objective Quadratic Programming (QP) tasks, the framework reduces theoretical complexity from O(n3) to O(n). Evaluations across 672 scenarios show an average decision latency of 7.58 s, a 2.9-fold efficiency gain over conventional methods. DAMPC achieved peak flow reductions of 16.26% and 18.72% compared to multi-objective and static benchmarks, respectively. Under ±30% precipitation uncertainty, the system maintains robustness with operational variations within tolerances. By combining computational speed with iterative refinement, DAMPC provides a practical decision-support tool for real-time flood control in complex urban water systems.
