AB  - This study proposes a novel integrated model, AuGPatt-BiLSTM, to address low accuracy in predicting extreme streamflow. The model integrates Complete Ensemble Empirical Mode Decomposition with Adaptive Noise (CEEMDAN) for decomposing series into Intrinsic Mode Functions (IMFs), autoencoders for denoising, and attention-enhanced Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (BiLSTM) optimized by a hybrid Gravitational Search Algorithm (GSA) and Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO). Using daily Yellow River flows (1962–2020), CEEMDAN captures nonlinearity and autoencoders preserve high-frequency information. GSA-PSO optimizes BiLSTM hyperparameters to eliminate manual tuning, while attention targets extreme events. Results show the Nash–Sutcliffe efficiency (NSE) reached 0.90 and 0.88 at Tongguan and Huaxian stations, respectively. Compared to traditional BiLSTM, the Mean Absolute Relative Error (MARE) for peak flows decreased by 41% and 28%. Furthermore, the model's generalizability was validated using datasets from the United States (Jacob Fork and Animas River), confirming its applicability to varying watershed scales.
