AB  - Nature-based solutions (NbS) are vital for sustainable flood risk management. However, integrating NbS into hydrological and hydrodynamic models is challenged by methodological and computational constraints. This study highlights NbS modelling challenges and offers technical recommendations for improved representation. NbS are categorized into landscape, storage, and diversion types, each requiring different modelling frameworks. Semi-distributed rainfall–runoff models suit preliminary assessments of landscape interventions, while storage and diversion measures demand high-resolution 2D hydrodynamic or Rain-on-Grid models to capture spatial inundation and flow dynamics. 1D hydrodynamic models may have significant limitations to simulate some nature-based solutions. Advancing NbS modelling calls for multi-dimensional, scale-aware frameworks with adaptive parameterization and dynamic mesh refinement to overcome data gaps and computational limits. Physical modelling and field studies remain essential for validating vegetation–hydraulics interactions. This synthesis provides a comprehensive framework and guidance for NbS simulation, highlighting future priorities.
