AB  - Large language models (LLMs) offer new opportunities, but their application in hydrological modelling remains limited. This study introduces the TIME-LLM framework, using structured prompts to guide an LLM in forecasting daily reservoir inflow. The prompts include task instructions, dataset statistics, and dataset context based on runoff yield and concentration mechanisms that incorporate climatic conditions, underlying surface characteristics, and human activities. Results show that the optimal prompt reduced the model's MAE and RMSE during the calibration period by 17.67% and 16.59%, respectively, while increasing NSE and KGE by 4.18% and 2.69%. During the validation period, MAE and RMSE decreased by 19.88% and 13.83%, respectively, while NSE and KGE improved by 7.29% and 0.34%. TIME-LLM captures temporal patterns in inflow series and recognizes hydrological prompts with reliability. These findings demonstrate the feasibility of applying LLMs to hydrological forecasting and suggest that prompt-based modelling may represent a new paradigm for data-driven environmental modelling.
